# koto artifact manifest — the trust anchor for `make fetch` and `make verify`.
#
# Plain sha256sum(1) format over the artifacts AS INSTALLED, relative to the
# repo root, so `sha256sum -c dist/artifacts.sha256` works verbatim and one
# file serves both acquisition routes: it checks a download, and it checks
# whether building from source reproduced the published bytes.
#
# This file is COMMITTED ON PURPOSE. It is what makes `make fetch` safe: the
# checksums arrive over git, not over the same connection as the artifacts
# they vouch for, so a compromised mirror cannot vouch for itself.
#
# Not every artifact reproduces bit-for-bit. koto and koto-tui do — they are
# CGO_ENABLED=0, -trimpath, built in a digest-pinned image. fcassets/vmlinux
# and fcassets/rootfs.img embed build timestamps and resolved package
# versions and do not; a mismatch on those after `make build` is expected.
#
# Entries look like:
#   <sha256>  koto
#   <sha256>  koto-tui
#   <sha256>  fcassets/firecracker
#   <sha256>  fcassets/vmlinux
#   <sha256>  fcassets/rootfs.img
#
# Empty until the first release is published. Until then DIST_VERSION in the
# Makefile reads "unreleased" and `make fetch` says so rather than 404ing, and
# `koto install` treats a tree with no entries here as pre-release.
#
# `make dist` regenerates the entries below from the build it packages.
